.TH CCHAN_DISABLE_SENDING 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cchan_disable_sending \- turn off sends on a cchan direction
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval cchan_disable_sending(cchan *ch, cchan_direction d);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Refuses every new send on the given direction and wakes every thread
that is blocked inside a send on that direction.

From then on the queue of that direction counts as ready for a write
selectable of cselect() and for a write registration of a
ceventloop, the same way a pipe whose reader has gone is ready for
POLLOUT. The send that the waiter makes after that wake returns
ccol_not_permitted at once. ccircq_disable_sending(3) has the
details.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
ch   Channel to change
d    Direction that gets sends turned off (cchan_owner_to_workers or
     cchan_workers_to_owner)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success       - Sends are off
ccol_invalid_args  - ch is NULL, or d is not valid
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Only the given direction changes; the other one keeps its state. To
turn sends back on, call cchan_enable_sending().
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cchan_enable_sending (3)
